WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.323
The metrics that matter.

2
00:00:01.603 --> 00:00:02.349
Cost.

3
00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:11.355
Chart cost per request and per completed task, broken down by feature, model, and prompt version, plus total daily spend against a budget.

4
00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:19.933
Cost per completed task is the one that guides decisions: a cheaper model that needs two retries or a human fix isn’t cheaper.

5
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:35.509
Compute cost from token counts and a versioned price table, and reconcile weekly against the provider’s own usage reporting: Anthropic’s docs note that exported spans are delivered on a best-effort basis, so a sum over spans can undercount.

6
00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:37.197
Latency percentiles.

7
00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:41.467
Use p50, p95, and p99, not the mean.

8
00:00:41.747 --> 00:00:50.344
The SRE book’s point is that an average hides a slow tail; record latency in histogram buckets so you can compute any percentile later.

9
00:00:50.624 --> 00:01:04.277
Chart time to first token separately for streamed responses, and track latency of failed requests separately too: the book notes that a slow error is even worse than a fast one, and timeouts are slow errors by definition.

10
00:01:04.557 --> 00:01:05.944
Error rates, by type.

11
00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:09.360
Split them, because each type has a different owner and fix.

12
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:16.488
Rate limiting (HTTP 429 on Claude) means you need more capacity or better backoff.

13
00:01:16.768 --> 00:01:24.917
Provider overload (529, overloaded_error, on Claude) means retries with jitter and possibly a fallback model.

14
00:01:25.197 --> 00:01:29.272
Timeouts may mean max_tokens is set too high for a synchronous path.

15
00:01:29.552 --> 00:01:35.739
Your own validation failures (a structured output that parses but fails your checks) point at the prompt.

16
00:01:36.019 --> 00:01:37.725
Refusals and truncations.

17
00:01:38.005 --> 00:01:40.821
These look like success at the HTTP layer.

18
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:55.288
Claude reports stop_reason: "refusal" (with a category in stop_details ) and max_tokens when output was cut off; other providers have equivalents, such as the refusal field in OpenAI’s structured outputs.

19
00:01:55.568 --> 00:01:57.253
Track each as a rate per route.

20
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:03.037
A refusal rate that doubles after a prompt change is a regression even though nothing errored.

21
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:04.405
Quality signals.

22
00:02:04.685 --> 00:02:17.720
The share of sampled answers that pass your graders (next section), user feedback rates, and task-level outcomes where you have them (a ticket resolved without escalation, a drafted reply sent without edits).

23
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.291
Tool-level signals matter for agents: tool error rate, calls per task, and loop length.

24
00:02:23.571 --> 00:02:31.272
Break every metric down by the dimensions you recorded in lesson 7: model, prompt version, route, and customer tier.

25
00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:36.395
A global average can stay flat while one prompt version on one route falls apart.
